---
import { MANAGEMENT_TOOLS, SUBAGENT_MAX_ATTEMPTS } from '../../consts/product';
import { numberWord } from '../../lib/format';
import Section from '../Section.astro';
---

<Section id="production" heading="Built for production, not just for the demo" bordered>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Background work is where the hard problems live: processes die, jobs get delivered twice, two workers grab the same task.
    The package handles them so you don't have to.
  </Fragment>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Nothing is lost when a process dies.</strong> A scheduled <em>sweep</em> runs every minute and starts again any
      work whose process disappeared. It executes nothing itself, it only rings the bell again.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Sub-agents resume where they stopped.</strong> A sub-agent whose process crashed is picked up by another one and
      continues its own conversation, up to {numberWord(SUBAGENT_MAX_ATTEMPTS)} attempts.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>A tool call never runs twice.</strong> If a background tool call crashes halfway, the package can't know whether it
      already took effect (did the email go out?), so it reports the task as failed instead of running it again.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Duplicates are harmless.</strong> A signal delivered twice, late, or to two workers at once changes nothing: only
      one of them does the work, and the result is reported once.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Approval happens before the work starts.</strong> A tool that needs a human's approval asks for it in the
      conversation, before the task is created, never in a process nobody is watching.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Stop when you want.</strong> <code class="ic">{MANAGEMENT_TOOLS.stop}</code> ends a task at its next step, and a
      stopped task reports nothing.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Each conversation sees only its own tasks.</strong> The toolkit works for the conversation your application bound
      the agent to: the model can never look at, or stop, another conversation's tasks.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>No new tables.</strong> Everything is kept in the stores your agents already use: Neuron's workflow persistence and
      message store, on a database or Redis.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Your infrastructure, your choice.</strong> Start tasks as local PHP processes with zero setup, or send them to any
      job queue by implementing one method.
    </li>
    <li class="t-body">
      <strong>Easy to test.</strong> <code class="ic">FakeRunner</code> keeps the work in memory and runs it when your test says
      so, so you can test the full round trip, from the tool call to the agent's answer, in one PHPUnit test.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Section>

<style lang="scss">
  .facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(var(--grid-min-list), 100%), 1fr));
    gap: 0 var(--space-12);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;

    li {
      padding-block: var(--space-5);
      border-top: var(--border-width) solid var(--color-line);
    }
  }
</style>
